// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`WORKFLOW_PRIMER (workflow_primer verb) > is deterministic across repeated reads 1`] = `
"agent-tasks workflow primer. See also docs/response-contract-v1.md, the normative contract this text summarizes.

## Lifecycle
Per-state agent guidance from this project's own workflow when it defines one, or the built-in default below otherwise:

- Open (open): Claim this task, create a branch, then transition to in_progress.
- In progress (in_progress): Implement the changes. When done, push the branch, create a PR, update prUrl and branchName, then transition to review.
- In review (review): Review is a code-review state. Approve or request changes here. Merge, deploy, and production verification are external follow-up actions unless your project defines a custom workflow for them.
- Done (done): Task is complete. Merge, deploy, and production verification are operational follow-ups outside the modeled task states unless a custom workflow models them explicitly.

## Canonical flows
Work: task_pickup -> task_start (author-claim, open -> in_progress) -> implement the change, run \`gh pr create\` -> task_submit_pr (records branchName/prUrl/prNumber) -> task_finish (advances to review or done, depending on the workflow).
Review: task_pickup (kind: "review") -> task_start (review-claim, no state change) -> task_finish { outcome: "approve" | "request_changes" }. approve moves the task to done; request_changes returns it to in_progress and keeps the work claim for the original author.

## Claim model
task_start (and task_pickup's review-claim) fails with 409 if you already hold an active claim on another task. task_finish, task_submit_pr, and task_abandon require that same claim, not just a taskId, and fail with 403 if you do not hold it.

## Receipt defaults (no include passed)
- task_pickup: the full task spec, without comments (the one write-verb exception; delivering the spec is its purpose).
- task_start: a receipt plus a small per-task slice (inferredTaskType, expectedFinishState, gateExpectations, gateExpectationsSource when assumed). Not the full task.
- task_create, task_respec, task_finish, task_submit_pr, task_merge, task_abandon, task_note: a small receipt only, shaped { ok, task: { id, status? }, transition?, deviations?, next? }. Fields you sent are never echoed back; deviations appear only when something needs your attention.

Pass include:["task"] on any of the verbs above to get the full, pre-contract object back instead (recovery path after context loss, or when you need the whole object in one call). task_start additionally accepts include:["description" | "instructions" | "comments"] to add back one field at a time, without paying for the rest.

## Errors today
A call that cannot proceed throws an MCP tool error (isError: true) whose text is \`agent-tasks API <status>: <backend message>\`, forwarded from the backend rather than a structured object yet. Known traps: acting without an active claim (403, call task_start first); an unmet workflow gate on task_finish (422 precondition_failed, listing the failing rules); already holding a claim on another task (409 on task_pickup/task_start, call task_finish or task_abandon on it first); a prUrl outside this project's linked repo on task_submit_pr (400 cross_repo_pr_rejected); tasks_transition with force=true as a non-admin (403); task_respec on a task that is claimed or not open (409). A structured teaching-error shape (code, message, recipe, allowedNext) is planned for a future release; until then, read the message text for the corrective call.

## Project rules
Call projects_get_effective_gates before task_create or task_submit_pr to see which gates are active on this project and why, instead of discovering them by tripping a 4xx."
`;
